{"query_id": "multihop_002", "category": "multi_hop", "query": "What is virtual memory, how does paging work, and why are page faults needed?", "model_key": "OLLAMA_BASELINE", "provider": "ollama", "requested_model": "qwen2.5:1.5b", "actual_model": "qwen2.5:1.5b", "request_id": "N/A", "status_code": 200, "prompt_sha256": "f14941adc5f49f7ecb4c01c87c609ef61014a89e10927081a1515eb47467031b", "context_sha256": "27b5e246783c2bff87630953586bc35b425a3b8267c13b438b93b489404db578", "context_chunk_count": 4, "context_documents": ["sample_exam.txt", "sample_exam_inspect.txt", "OS_Notes.txt"], "retrieval_latency_sec": 3.1601, "generation_latency_sec": 3.1229, "wall_latency_sec": 6.283, "prompt_tokens": 0, "completion_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 399, "full_answer": "Virtual memory allows secondary storage to be accessed as if it were in RAM. Paging works by dividing the address space into fixed-size segments called pages. When a program attempts to access a page that is not currently in RAM, a page fault occurs, indicating that the required data must be loaded from disk before execution can continue.\n\nPage faults are needed because they allow programs to request and receive memory addresses for which there is no corresponding physical address in main memory. This ensures that all parts of a program's code and data are always accessible when needed, even if some portions have not yet been loaded into RAM.", "answer_length_chars": 650, "error": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-13 17:12:39"}
