{"query_id": "multihop_002", "category": "multi_hop", "query": "What is virtual memory, how does paging work, and why are page faults needed?", "model_key": "OPENROUTER_MODEL_B", "provider": "openrouter", "requested_model": "nvidia/nemotron-nano-9b-v2:free", "actual_model": "nvidia/nemotron-nano-9b-v2:free", "request_id": "gen-1786621393-PQBa2w73zH8qmw32VNi9", "status_code": 200, "prompt_sha256": "f14941adc5f49f7ecb4c01c87c609ef61014a89e10927081a1515eb47467031b", "context_sha256": "27b5e246783c2bff87630953586bc35b425a3b8267c13b438b93b489404db578", "context_chunk_count": 4, "context_documents": ["sample_exam.txt", "sample_exam_inspect.txt", "OS_Notes.txt"], "retrieval_latency_sec": 3.1601, "generation_latency_sec": 11.2015, "wall_latency_sec": 14.3616, "prompt_tokens": 269, "completion_tokens": 425, "total_tokens": 694, "full_answer": "Answer:  \nVirtual memory is a memory management technique that creates an illusion of a large main memory by using secondary storage (e.g., disk) to extend the available memory space. Paging works by dividing memory into fixed-size blocks called pages, which can be stored in either RAM or secondary memory. When a process accesses a page not currently in RAM, a page fault occurs, prompting the operating system to load the required page from secondary storage into RAM. Page faults are necessary to manage memory efficiently, allowing programs to use more memory than physically available in RAM while ensuring data integrity and optimal resource utilization.", "answer_length_chars": 661, "error": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-13 17:13:24"}
{"query_id": "multihop_004", "category": "multi_hop", "query": "How do page tables and TLBs interact to accelerate memory reference translation?", "model_key": "OLLAMA_BASELINE", "provider": "ollama", "requested_model": "qwen2.5:1.5b", "actual_model": "qwen2.5:1.5b", "request_id": "N/A", "status_code": 200, "prompt_sha256": "0c47be47dbbd6a226531902cd4c5ce990ef40e0448177325e727ab717a3eaa78", "context_sha256": "aa645949367d70da52bce34e3d0a6966490a41520d94fcdc2bf5ae80f84bbd57", "context_chunk_count": 4, "context_documents": ["sample_exam.txt", "sample_routing_doc.txt", "sample_exam_inspect.txt"], "retrieval_latency_sec": 2.9689, "generation_latency_sec": 3.1997, "wall_latency_sec": 6.1686, "prompt_tokens": 0, "completion_tokens": 0, "total_tokens": 422, "full_answer": "Page tables and Translation Lookaside Buffers (TLBs) work together to accelerate the process of translating virtual addresses into physical addresses in a multi-level addressing system.\n\n- Page Tables:\n  - Store mappings between virtual pages and physical frames.\n  - Used for direct mapping, where each page is mapped directly to its corresponding frame on the main memory.\n\n- TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer):\n  - A small cache that holds recently used page table entries.\n  - Accelerates the translation process by reducing the number of times a page table lookup must be performed.\n\nTogether, they allow for faster and more efficient mapping of virtual addresses to physical addresses, significantly improving system performance.", "answer_length_chars": 735, "error": null, "timestamp": "2026-08-13 17:13:30"}
